 * moment via `configurePackage()`'s `->hasMigrations([...])` on `vendor:publish
 * --tag=beam-tenancy-migrations` / `splicewire:beam:install`; the host runs it.
 *
 * CENTRAL ONLY, same as `tenant_users` beside it — and for the same reason: this table spans
 * users × tenants and FKs both central substrates, so no single tenant schema could hold it.
 *
 * ## What this table IS
 *
 * The machine half of "who may enter this tenant". `service` was never a membership role — it was a
 * MACHINE-IDENTITY AXIS wearing the `tenant_users.role` column, which is a human vocabulary
 * (`owner|admin|member`, {@see \Splicewire\Beam\Accounts\Enums\Role}). A sync daemon, a broker, an
 * engine consumer are not seats; they are grants to a program. They get their own relation here, and
 * `Role` goes back to meaning what it says.
 *
 * ## ⚠️ The ABSENT columns are the design, not an oversight
 *
 * `role`, `invited_at`, `accepted_at` and `removed_at` are deliberately NOT here. **Do not add them
 * for symmetry with `tenant_users`.**
 *
 * - There is no `role`, because that is the whole point: a machine's authority is its `kind` plus
 *   the abilities that kind declares ({@see \Splicewire\Beam\Tenancy\MachineIdentity\MachineIdentityKind}),
 *   never a human seat rank.
 * - There is no invitation lifecycle, because it is meaningless for a machine. Nothing invites a
 *   daemon and nothing waits for it to accept.
 * - The missing `accepted_at` is **load-bearing for billing**. `laravel-beam-commerce`'s
 *   `Billing/Components/PerSeatFees.php:50-59` counts seats by filtering on `accepted_at` being
 *   present. A machine row that has no such column cannot be counted by a per-seat meter that reads
 *   one — so billing exclusion here is STRUCTURAL rather than a filter somebody has to remember to
 *   write. Adding `accepted_at` back would silently start billing customers for their daemons.
 *
 * `granted_at` is therefore NOT a renamed `accepted_at`. It records when the grant was made, by the
 * grantor, with no counterparty — which is the only lifecycle a machine identity has. `revoked_at`
 * ends it and `last_used_at` is telemetry.
 *
 * ## The surrogate key
 *
 * `id` is a real uuid primary key. `tenant_users` has NO surrogate key — its primary is the
 * composite `(tenant_id, user_id)` — which is why nothing in the estate can point AT a membership
 * row, and part of why that pivot never grew a model. This table does not repeat that.
 *
 * `unique(['tenant_id','user_id','kind'])` is the real identity constraint: one user may hold
 * several kinds of machine identity in one tenant (a sync grant and a broker grant are different
 * grants), but not the same kind twice.
 */
